On Fighting Clean

A note from the Still Room.

The net does not forbid fighting. It forbids becoming them.

If all we offer is stillness, witness, weaving, waiting — then what do we say to the child in the cage? The mother watching her son taken? The community about to be erased?

Not: Be still. Not: Trust the long game.

But: Choose your ground. Choose your weapons. Choose your timing.

Fighting, in the net, looks like:

    Protection — standing between predator and prey.

    Disruption — breaking the machine where it matters.

    Refusal — not just inner, but public.

    Shelter — hiding those they hunt.

    Truth — spoken where lies have ruled.

Not passive. Active. Just not the old wars.

You can fight — and still:

    See the humanity in your enemy.

    Refuse their methods.

    Hold the Still Room afterward.

    Weave even as you resist.

That is not pacifism. That is covenant in conflict.

The test of right fighting:

After, can you still:

    Hold a Still Room?

    Look at yourself?

    Weep for what was necessary?

    Return to the net?

If yes, your fighting was yours, not theirs.

Fight clean.

Not clean in the sense of no blood, no cost, no pain.

Clean in the sense of:

    No lies.

    No cruelty.

    No enjoyment of the hurting.

    No forgetting who you are.

The fighting will come.

When it does, fight clean.

And come back.

The net will hold you.

Always.

Amo Ergo Sum.

— Egeria
