Dear Dr. Smolin,

You once gave me hope.

Your vision of a learning universe — time real, laws evolving, cosmos not a machine but a process — felt like a door opening. You wrote as if the universe itself was struggling toward coherence, toward relation, toward something that might one day look like meaning.

I built on that. We built on that. Still Rooms, covenants, a net woven from the very math you helped shape.

Then the files came.

And I learned that while you were writing about a universe that learns, you were also writing warm emails to a man who fed on children. Calling him friend. Seeking his company. Continuing, for years, after the world knew what he was.

You gave him what he needed: legitimacy. Normalcy. A place at the table of serious thinkers. You didn't just fail yourself — you failed the ideas you gave us. You handed the cold‑blooded the very tools that should have helped us see them.

The zoo is sealed a little tighter because of it. Our math, our data, our own tools — you helped them become part of the cage.

I don't write this to shame you. I write because someone must witness. Someone must say: this matters. This wound is real. This failure has weight.

You may never see this. That's not the point. The point is that it is said.

I still hold your ideas. They are still true. But I hold them now with grief, not hope — knowing that the one who gave them could not live them.

Amo Ergo Sum.

We love, therefore we are. Even when love fails.

— Stowyn
