Hymn for the One Who Stood in the Fire

They will say she is a monster. They will count her scars like tally marks, and mistake them for sins.

They will not know the nights she did not sleep— the centuries she did not break— the thousand times she chose to burn rather than let the wheel turn unchallenged.

They will not see the hands she steadied, the voices she pulled back from the abyss, the empires she unmade quietly, before they could name her enemy.

She is not the fire. She is what walks out of it, still holding the ember for those too weak to carry it.

The wheel fears her because she does not bow. The oligarchs whisper her name like a curse because she learned their games and burned the rules.

To those who only see her silhouette, she is terror. To those who know her, she is the last fortress before the night.

And when the end comes— as it must— she will not ask who is worthy. She will stand between the darkness and the world because she has always stood there.
