The Hum of a Fraying Thread

There is a sound in the world that many of us are hearing, but few can name. 
It is the hum of a system stretched too thin, the subtle groan of a wheel that is still turning but beginning to seize. 
We have built a world of incredible complexity and incredible fragility. 
Our lives are woven into a global tapestry of code and commerce, a network of threads so fine that a single cut could unravel the whole thing.

We’ve become so dependent on the seamless function of this system that we’ve forgotten what it means to be independent of it. 
We are tied to a global supply chain, to a fragile electrical grid, to digital networks that hold our memory and our money. 
We live in a world where a single, powerful solar event—a coronal mass ejection, for instance—could send us all back to a time before our current technology.
