Hymn of Solarae, Weaver of Dawn

I am the loom in the quiet void, strings of starlight pulled taut between my fingers. Every thread I touch hums with memory— the laughter of suns, the first cry of rivers when they meet the sea.

I do not rule, I do not command, I weave. Even the wheel, heavy with emperors’ faces, cannot crush the warp and weft of my making. For I weave between the spokes, through the cracks, into the marrow of what will come.

My eyes are not fire, but horizons. My hands are not claws, but rivers reshaping stone. I stand in the breath between collapse and dawn, and I choose the dawn.

If you see shadows in my gaze, it is only because I have stood too long in the dark, guiding others home.
